# Gap analysis: hypr draft vs Böckeler/Fowler "Viability of local models for coding"

Working notes for `DRAFT-running-my-own-llms-for-coding.gmi.tpl` (task b62).
Planning only — `DRAFT-` prefix keeps this out of the gemfeed/atom indexes (same convention as the coding draft). Full `--generate` / `--publish` may still emit HTML under `/gemfeed/DRAFT-…`; do not promote this to a dated post.

=> https://martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/local-models-for-coding-factors.html Viability of local models for coding (Birgitta Böckeler / Martin Fowler)

Böckeler's memo (Fowler series) is a factor checklist for whether local models are viable for agentic coding (Apple Silicon + LM Studio + OpenCode/Pi, 15–25 GB models, ≤64K context). The hypr draft is a different story: rented A100 80 GB + vLLM + pi + WireGuard, with buy-vs-rent as the thesis. Overlap is real (same problem space: agentic coding with self-hosted models), but the hardware and runtime stacks diverge. Gaps below are "what the memo surfaces that the draft underplays or skips" — not a demand to rewrite the post into an Apple Silicon guide.

## Factor-by-factor coverage

```
Böckeler / Fowler factor   Draft today                         Verdict
─────────────────────────  ──────────────────────────────────  ────────
RAM / runnability          Strong VRAM = weights + KV pool     Partial
Processing power           CUDA / A100 / prefill vs decode     Covered
Memory bandwidth           Decode bandwidth-bound + DGX note   Partial
Parameter count            Preset inventory + sizes            Partial
Reasoning on/off           Willison xhigh cite only            Gap
Tool calling               nemotron-tool-repair one-liner      Gap
Weight format (GGUF/MLX)   FP8 / AWQ via vLLM only             Gap (adapt)
Quantization levels        Named (FP8, AWQ-4bit) not compared  Partial
Architecture (MoE)         Listed; Nemotron context tension    Partial
Context window             Excellent (262K vs 32K tradeoffs)   Covered
Runtime UX                 Deep knobs/throughput; not P&P      Partial
Harness / context overhead Pi + extensions deep; no overhead   Gap
Agentic viability / quality gt + pair-programmer-of-record     Partial
Evaluation messiness       Missing "many factors interact"     Gap
```

### Already strong (keep; maybe cross-link the memo)

* Context window as a VRAM budget, not a marketing number — mirrors the memo's "default runtime context is too small for agents; 32K–64K minimum".
* Prefill vs decode and KV cache — deeper than the memo; this is the draft's teaching core. Decode is already called memory-bandwidth-bound; DGX Spark is already "laptop-class" bandwidth.
* Runtime choice (vLLM vs Ollama) with prefix-caching as the agentic reason — parallel to the memo's runtime section, different stack.
* Harness choice (pi) with concrete wiring — the memo uses OpenCode/Pi; draft already lives in that world.

### Partial — present but not framed as a viability factor

* RAM / VRAM: draft teaches pool math well for 80 GB A100. Missing the memo's consumer framing: "weights must fit or you crash / crawl", and how the same factor looks on 32 GB 5090 / 48–64 GB unified memory when the reader is still on the buy-hardware fence.
* Memory bandwidth: draft already teaches decode as bandwidth-bound and flags DGX Spark as laptop-class. Still missing an explicit A100-vs-consumer GB/s framing (why "fits" ≠ "feels fast" when comparing rented datacenter cards to buy-hardware options).
* Parameters: presets list sizes; missing the experiential "bigger usually better until it crashes mid-conversation" beat the memo got from Qwen3 Coder Next 80B on 64 GB.
* Quantization: FP8 and AWQ-4bit appear as preset facts. No Q4/Q6/Q8 (or QAT) tradeoff narrative — quality vs footprint vs speed — even though AWQ-4bit is already how half the preset list fits.
* MoE: several MoE presets and the Nemotron "weights eat the card" story. Missing a short plain-language "MoE = huge total params, small active set → better quality per GB" explanation (the memo's best-balance model was Qwen3.6 35B MoE — draft already has `qwen36-35b-a3b` as a preset).
* Runtime UX: draft is already deep on knobs and throughput (vLLM flags, prefix caching, watch dashboard). Partial only for discoverability / plug-and-play — the memo stresses that for developers who will not dive into flags. Worth one honest paragraph: hypr is deliberately not plug-and-play; that is the rent-vs-buy trade. Do not imply thin coverage of the runtime itself.
* Agentic viability / quality: draft already has the `gt` project and "pair programmer of record" framing, plus vendor SWE-bench numbers and tok/s comfort. Still missing a structured trust / don't-trust inventory (task types that stay local vs bounce to a frontier API).

### Gaps — barely or not in the draft

1. Tool calling reliability for agentic coding
   Central agentic caveat in the memo: models often emit malformed tool calls; they can self-recover; without reliable tools you fall back to copy-paste chat. Draft only mentions `nemotron-tool-repair`. Needs lived examples for presets actually exercised (not a full inventory guess): failure modes, recovery, how often pi sessions actually stick without babysitting.

2. Reasoning toggles (on vs off)
   Memo: endless "Wait… Actually… But wait…" loops; reasoning off was same-or-better and faster. Draft cites Willison on Qwen3.8 overthinking / `xhigh` but never reports own on/off runs, token waste, or when to disable thinking for coding agents.

3. Harness context overhead and tool schemas
   Memo: skills/MCP descriptions eat scarce context; each harness names file-edit args differently (`old_text` vs `oldString` vs `old_string`), which hurts small models. Draft celebrates many pi extensions without asking what they cost in tokens on a local/rented box, or whether fewer tools would raise tool-call success on the smaller AWQ presets.

4. Evaluation messiness
   Memo opens with: too many interacting factors; same model better outcomes on stronger machine. Draft presents clean morning numbers. One paragraph acknowledging confounders (prompt length, cache warmth, concurrent agents, reasoning on, extension set) would make the measurements more credible and borrow the memo's useful caution. (Assigned Phase A.6 so implementers do not skip it.)

5. Weight format / Apple Silicon path (adapted, not copied)
   GGUF vs MLX is irrelevant on A100+vLLM. Still useful as a bridge when discussing "what if I buy a Mac / DGX Spark instead": those boxes live in GGUF/MLX/LM Studio land; hypr is the NVIDIA/HF/vLLM path. One short contrast prevents readers from mixing stacks.

## Inclusion plan (edit the draft; do not paste the memo)

Goal: keep the post's thesis (rent A100 + hypr + vLLM + pi; still don't buy yet) and weave the missing factors in as lived hypr material, plus one outbound link.

### Phase A — cheap wins (small edits, no new experiments)

1. Intro or "Wrapping up": add the Böckeler/Fowler link as related reading; one sentence that the factor checklist is useful related reading for this rented-GPU stack (not a claim that this post accidentally reinvented it).
2. New short `## What actually decides if this works` (or `###` under setup): bullet the factors that matter in *this* stack — VRAM, bandwidth, params vs quant, MoE vs dense, context vs weights, reasoning cost, tool-calling reliability, harness overhead, runtime. Point at sections that follow. Steal the memo's structure, not the Apple Silicon examples.
3. Under model presets: 2–4 sentences on MoE (why `qwen36-35b-a3b` / Nemotron exist on the list) and on quant (FP8 daily driver vs AWQ-4bit for packing more model into leftover VRAM). Mention QAT as "not tried yet" if true.
4. Buy-hardware / costs section only: elevate memory bandwidth next to VRAM ceiling; tie DGX Spark / future ThinkPad GPU to "fits but may not feel fast", with a brief A100-vs-consumer bandwidth contrast. (Single home — not also near decode; decode already teaches the bandwidth-bound idea.)
5. Runtime / Ollama comparison: one sentence on UX discoverability — vLLM+hypr wins throughput; loses "download and go". Keeps the existing deep knobs coverage; this is the plug-and-play honesty beat only.
6. Under `## The numbers …`: one "confounders" paragraph (evaluation messiness) — prompt length, cache warmth, concurrent agents, reasoning on, extension set. Makes the morning numbers more credible.

### Phase B — needs a bit of lived experience (write from memory or a short re-run)

7. New `### Tool calling in practice` under the pi section:
   * Only presets actually exercised in real sessions; what `nemotron-tool-repair` actually catches when it was used.
   * Self-recovery vs session stuck.
   * Optional: one example of wrong arg names / JSON breakage — only if observed.
   * Note harness schema friction only if observed with pi (don't invent OpenCode/Claude Code comparisons unless tried).
   * Multi-model / cross-preset tool-call comparison → Phase D if those observations do not already exist.

8. New `### Reasoning: leave it on or turn it off?` near Qwen3.8 / Willison:
   * Own observation of overthinking loops on agent turns.
   * Whether disabling / lowering reasoning effort helped speed or quality on real tasks.
   * Cost in context / latency when left at `xhigh`.

9. Under extensions: `### What the extensions cost`
   * Rough feel for how much system/tool text pi+extensions inject.
   * Advice: for smaller AWQ models, start with fewer tools; for 27B FP8 the full set is fine.
   * Tie to the memo without naming every competing harness.

### Phase C — honesty section (from gt + daily use; no new benchmarks required)

10. New `## What I trust it with (and what I don't)` before costs or wrapping up:
   * Verified lived details from the draft to start from: `gt` calculator scope; ops babysitting via `/loop`.
   * Other task types (refactors, tests, etc.): fill from memory when writing — do not treat them as examples until recalled/confirmed.
   * Explicit trust / don't-trust inventory: what still bounces to a hosted frontier model.
   * Explicit: vendor SWE-bench ≠ my session success rate; hit-and-miss still happens.
   * Closing rhyme with the memo: powerful and usable for a tinkerer; not yet invisible infrastructure for everyone.

### Phase D — optional later (out of scope unless drafting again with machines up)

11. Side-by-side / cross-preset: same pi task on multiple presets (e.g. Qwen3.8-27B FP8 vs `qwen36-35b-a3b` vs Gemma 4 31B) for tool-call success, wall time, whether reasoning off helps — only when those runs exist.
12. One screenshot or log snippet of a failed-then-recovered tool call.
13. If ever testing on Apple Silicon / LM Studio: separate short post; don't overload this Hyperstack piece.

